  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nCurrently, an upper level ridge is centered over the Southern High \nPlains. Troughing over the East Coast has kept the ridge (and the \nwarmest temperatures) slightly further west. A weak shortwave is \nslowly moving southeast over Kansas with significant moisture \nadvection ahead of the wave. PWATs in excess of 2\" extend across \nmuch of the forecast area along with MLCAPE values between 1500-2000 \nJ/kg across central Kansas. This environment, combined with the \nshortwave, has been conducive for shower and weak thunderstorm \ndevelopment. This activity will gradually shift eastward through the \nafternoon and evening hours, though no severe weather is anticipated \ngiven the lack of shear. The moisture advection and lift has also \nlead to a decent cloud shield that has limited our high temperatures \nacross central and south-central Kansas. Though, with temperatures \nin the lower to middle 90s and dewpoints in the 70s, heat index \nvalues across southern Kansas will still manage to reach into the \n100-106 degree range. A Heat Advisory remains in effect through 9PM.\n\nFor tonight through Friday, as the forecast area continues to sit on \nthe northeastern edge of the mid/upper ridge, small perturbations in \nthe flow will lead to repeated rain and storm chances. Diurnal \nconvection that develops on the High Plains later this afternoon \nwill travel into central Kansas later tonight into the morning \nhours, gradually pushing east into eastern Kansas on Thursday \nmorning/early afternoon. Due to the overall warm column, severe \nweather is not anticipated with this activity. However, when a more \npotent shortwave passes across the Northern Plains into the Upper \nMidwest on Thursday night, a weak frontal boundary will sweep across \nthe state. This setup will support more widespread showers and \nstorms into Friday morning. While severe weather is not widely \nanticipated, the synoptic forcing combined with the moderately \nunstable environment may support some stronger wind gusts and heavy \nrain. \n\nTemperatures through the end of the work week will remain a few \ndegrees above normal for this time of year as we remain under the \ninfluence of the ridge. For Thursday, highs in the middle to upper \n90s will be most likely across central and south-central Kansas \nwhere temperatures will have a chance to rebound behind morning \nconvection. Eastern Kansas will max out in the lower 90s. Then for \nFriday, locations across the northern portion of the forecast area \nwill begin to see the impacts from the frontal passage as \ntemperatures will only reach into the upper 80s to lower 90s. \nWhereas far southern Kansas will see temperatures in the upper 90s \nto around 100 degrees. Peak heat index values will also reach into \nthe 100-105 degree range for many across the forecast area. We will \ncontinue to not receive much relief during the overnight hours as \nlows will only fall into the middle 70s each night. \n\nThen for Saturday through the end of the forecast period, slightly \nbelow normal temperatures are expected this weekend behind the \n\"cold\" front. The ridge will be further shunted to the west and the \nforecast area will be under northerly flow. Afternoon highs across \nthe region look to reach into the upper 80s to lower 90s with \novernight lows falling into the 60s. Heights then look to gradually \nincrease for early next week with temperatures creeping back into \nthe lower to middle 90s in response. A much drier airmass will also \nfilter in along with the more mild temperatures with PWATs around 1\" \nfor this weekend into early next week. This will limit rain chances \nand lead to mild RH values in the 30% range, leading to heat indices \nmuch closer to actual air temperature.",
